In this lesson we'll look at Pythagoras' theorem and how it shows up in three‑dimensional or spatial problems. The core idea is simple. Whenever you have a right‑angled triangle, the longest side, called the hypotenuse, is the one sitting opposite the right angle. The theorem tells you that the square of that longest side equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides. So if you know any two sides, you can always find the third by adding or subtracting those squares and taking a square root. Now, the trick in spatial problems is spotting where the right angle is hiding. It might be the diagonal cutting across the base of a box, the slant running up a ramp, or the gap between two points on a grid. Once you spot that hidden right triangle, Pythagoras does the rest. Let's see it in action.